    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>Lisbon grew from an <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iron-age">Iron Age</a> settlement on the Castelo hill that traded with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phoenicians">Phoenicians</a> into the Roman municipium of Olisipo and, after the 8th century, the Muslim city of al-Us̲h̲būna, which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afonso-i-of-portugal">Afonso I</a> conquered in 1147.</li><li>It became the capital of Portugal in the 13th century and, from the end of the 15th century, the port from which most Portuguese expeditions set out, trading in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spice-trade">spices</a>, slaves, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sugar-plantation">sugar</a> and textiles between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, the Far East and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brazil">Brazil</a>.</li><li>The earthquake of 1 November 1755 destroyed 85 percent of its buildings, and the lower town was rebuilt on a rectangular plan under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marquis-of-pombal">Marquis of Pombal</a>.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>Pombal's planners tested architectural models for the rebuilt Baixa by having troops march around them to simulate an earthquake.</p></aside>
    </section>

